const esdOsQs=[
{q:"What is the primary responsibility of an operating system?",o:["Manage hardware resources and provide services to programs","Design electronic circuits","Translate analog signals","Create CPU transistors"],a:0,e:"An operating system manages resources such as CPU, memory and devices, and provides services to application software."},
{q:"Which characteristic is essential in a real-time operating system (RTOS)?",o:["Predictable response within timing constraints","Maximum graphical effects","Unlimited memory","No interrupt support"],a:0,e:"An RTOS is designed for predictable timing behaviour so tasks can meet their deadlines."},
{q:"What is a task in an RTOS?",o:["A schedulable unit of execution","A physical GPIO pin","A type of resistor","A compiler warning"],a:0,e:"A task or thread is an independent unit of execution managed by the RTOS scheduler."},
{q:"What does a scheduler decide in a multitasking operating system?",o:["Which ready task gets CPU time","Which source file is deleted","The physical size of RAM","The voltage of a GPIO pin"],a:0,e:"The scheduler selects the next task to execute based on the scheduling policy and task states."},
{q:"In a priority-based preemptive RTOS, what normally happens when a higher-priority task becomes ready?",o:["It can preempt a lower-priority running task","It is always ignored","The system must shut down","All tasks are deleted"],a:0,e:"A preemptive priority scheduler can suspend a lower-priority task to run a newly ready higher-priority task."},
{q:"What is a context switch?",o:["Saving one task's execution state and restoring another's","Changing a C variable into a pointer","Powering off the microcontroller","Formatting flash memory"],a:0,e:"A context switch saves the current task state and restores the state of the task selected to run."},
{q:"What is a semaphore commonly used for in an RTOS?",o:["Synchronizing tasks or controlling access to a resource","Converting analog signals","Increasing clock frequency","Storing program code permanently"],a:0,e:"Semaphores coordinate task execution and can signal events or manage access to shared resources."},
{q:"What is a mutex primarily designed to provide?",o:["Mutual exclusion when accessing a shared resource","Analog voltage measurement","Task compilation","Network address assignment"],a:0,e:"A mutex allows only one task at a time to hold a protected resource, helping prevent concurrent access conflicts."},
{q:"What is priority inversion?",o:["A high-priority task is delayed by a lower-priority task holding a needed resource","A task changes its name","The CPU reverses its instruction set","All priorities become equal permanently"],a:0,e:"Priority inversion occurs when a lower-priority task blocks a higher-priority task, often while holding a shared resource."},
{q:"Which technique can help mitigate priority inversion?",o:["Priority inheritance","Busy waiting everywhere","Disabling all timers","Increasing stack usage"],a:0,e:"Priority inheritance temporarily raises the priority of a resource-holding task to reduce blocking of higher-priority tasks."},
{q:"What is a deadlock?",o:["A set of tasks waits indefinitely for resources held by one another","A task completes normally","A timer reaches zero","A processor enters sleep mode briefly"],a:0,e:"Deadlock is a state where tasks cannot proceed because each is waiting for a resource or event that another task in the set must release."},
{q:"What is the purpose of an interrupt handler in an embedded OS?",o:["Respond to a hardware or software interrupt event","Allocate every file on disk","Replace the scheduler","Compile the application"],a:0,e:"An interrupt handler services an interrupt source, usually performing short time-critical work before returning."},
{q:"Why should lengthy processing generally be moved out of an interrupt handler?",o:["To keep interrupt latency and blocking time low","To use more stack memory","To stop task scheduling permanently","To make the interrupt occur twice"],a:0,e:"Short handlers allow the system to respond promptly to other interrupts and defer non-urgent work to tasks."},
{q:"What is a task's stack used for?",o:["Function calls, local variables and saved execution information","Storing all files on flash","Driving external motors directly","Replacing program memory"],a:0,e:"Each task commonly has its own stack for call frames, local data and context information."},
{q:"What can happen if an RTOS task exceeds its allocated stack space?",o:["Stack overflow and unpredictable system behaviour","Automatic increase in CPU speed","Guaranteed recovery without impact","The task becomes a hardware timer"],a:0,e:"Stack overflow can corrupt adjacent memory and cause crashes or unpredictable behaviour."},
{q:"What is inter-task communication?",o:["Exchanging data or signals between tasks","Connecting two power supplies in series","Changing the processor architecture","Converting source code to a PDF"],a:0,e:"RTOS mechanisms such as queues, mailboxes and message buffers let tasks exchange information safely."},
{q:"What is a message queue used for?",o:["Passing messages or data between tasks in a controlled order","Storing only executable machine code","Measuring analog voltage","Generating a CPU clock"],a:0,e:"A queue buffers messages so one task can send data and another can receive it, often asynchronously."},
{q:"What is the main purpose of an idle task in many RTOS designs?",o:["Run when no other ready task needs the CPU","Always preempt every task","Handle every hardware interrupt","Store the bootloader"],a:0,e:"The idle task executes when no other task is ready and may provide a place for low-priority background or power-management work."},
{q:"Which statement best describes a hard real-time system?",o:["Missing a critical deadline is considered system failure","Response time never matters","It must use a desktop operating system","It cannot use interrupts"],a:0,e:"In a hard real-time system, missing a required deadline is unacceptable and may constitute failure."},
{q:"What is the purpose of a bootloader in an embedded device?",o:["Initialize or load the main application and may support firmware updates","Schedule all RTOS tasks","Measure PWM duty cycle","Replace the device's RAM"],a:0,e:"A bootloader runs early in startup and can initialize the device, launch firmware or provide a firmware-update mechanism."}
];
